\\) for which \\(k = \\frac{1}{1+2x}\\) is a terminating decimal, where \\(x\\) is an integer greater than 1. A fraction is a terminating decimal if and only if the denominator in its simplest form has no prime factors other than 2 and 5.\n\nGiven:\n\\[ k = \\frac{1}{1+2x} \\]\n\nLet's denote \\(1 + 2x\\) by \\(d\\). Therefore,\n\\[ k = \\frac{1}{d} \\]\nwhere \\(d = 1 + 2x\\). Since \\(x > 1\\), we have \\(d > 3\\).\n\nFor \\(k\\) to be a terminating decimal, \\(d\\) must be of the form \\(2^a \\times 5^b\\) where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are non-negative integers.\n\nNow, let's list the possible values of \\(d\\) that are greater than 3 and are of the form \\(2^a \\times 5^b\\):\n- \\(d = 4 = 2^2 \\times 5^0\\)\n- \\(d = 5 = 2^0 \\times 5^1\\)\n- \\(d = 8 = 2^3 \\times 5^0\\)\n- \\(d = 10 = 2^1 \\times 5^1\\)\n- \\(d = 16 = 2^4 \\times 5^0\\)\n- \\(d = 20 = 2^2 \\times 5^1\\)\n- \\(d = 25 = 2^0 \\times 5^2\\)\n- \\(d = 32 = 2^5 \\times 5^0\\)\n- \\(d = 40 = 2^3 \\times 5^1\\)\n- \\(d = 50 = 2^1 \\times 5^2\\)\n- \\(d = 64 = 2^6 \\times 5^0\\)\n- \\(d = 80 = 2^4 \\times 5^1\\)\n- \\(d = 100 = 2^2 \\times 5^2\\)\n- \\(d = 125 = 2^0 \\times 5^3\\)\n- \\(d = 128 = 2^7 \\times 5^0\\)\n- \\(d = 160 = 2^5 \\times 5^1\\)\n- \\(d = 200 = 2^3 \\times 5^2\\)\n- \\(d = 250 = 2^1 \\times 5^3\\)\n- \\(d = 256 = 2^8 \\times 5^0\\)\n- \\(d = 320 = 2^6 \\times 5^1\\)\n- \\(d = 400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\)\n- \\(d = 500 = 2^2 \\times 5^3\\)\n- \\(d = 512 = 2^9 \\times 5^0\\)\n- \\(d = 640 = 2^7 \\times 5^1\\)\n- \\(d = 800 = 2^5 \\times 5^2\\)\n- \\(d = 1000 = 2^3 \\times 5^3\\)\n\nNow, let's find the corresponding values of \\(k\\) for these \\(d\\):\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{4}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{5}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{8}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{10}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{16}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{20}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{25}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{32}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{40}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{50}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{64}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{80}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{100}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{125}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{128}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{160}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{200}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{250}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{256}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{320}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{400}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{500}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{512}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{640}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{800}\\)\n- \\(k = \\frac{1}{1000}\\)\n\nThe sum of all these values is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{8} + \\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{25} + \\frac{1}{32} + \\frac{1}{40} + \\frac{1}{50} + \\frac{1}{64} + \\frac{1}{80} + \\frac{1}{100} + \\frac{1}{125} + \\frac{1}{128} + \\frac{1}{160} + \\frac{1}{200} + \\frac{1}{250} + \\frac{1}{256} + \\frac{1}{320} + \\frac{1}{400} + \\frac{1}{500} + \\frac{1}{512} + \\frac{1}{640} + \\frac{1}{800} + \\frac{1}{1000}\n\\]\n\nCalculating this sum gives:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\frac{1}{3}}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
